+++
title: Followership
hook: Can you fully back a decision that wasn't yours to make?
hook2: Or does not being the one in charge feel like not mattering?
+++

Almost everyone spends more of their life following than leading — on a team, in a family, in a community, in a partnership — and almost no one is taught how to do it well. Followership coaching is about the craft of supporting a direction you didn't set: how to commit without disappearing, how to disagree without undermining, and how to tell the difference between loyalty and compliance.

What do you owe the person leading, and what do they owe you? When is speaking up the most supportive thing you can do, and when is it just noise? How do you stay engaged with a decision you argued against? How do you choose who is worth following in the first place, and how do you know when it's time to stop?

Good followership is not passivity, and it is not a waiting room for leadership. It is its own skill, practiced deliberately, and the people who are best at it make everyone around them — including whoever is in charge — better.
